---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import MermaidDiagram from '../components/MermaidDiagram.astro';
import { selfImprovingDiagram } from '../data/diagrams';
---

<DocsLayout title="Self-Improving System" sectionLabel="How It Works" activeSlug="self-improving">

  <div class="panel-intro">
    <h2>Self-Improving System</h2>
    <p>VibeCrew doesn't just build software &mdash; it <strong>learns from every session</strong>. The Performance Coach analyzes each session's patterns, proposes permanent rule changes to CLAUDE.md, and aggregates anonymized metrics across all your projects. Each session makes the next one better.</p>
    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>What it is</strong>
          <p>A feedback loop: the Performance Coach scores each session, detects anti-patterns, and proposes CLAUDE.md mutations. Cross-project telemetry tracks trends across all your VibeCrew projects. The <code>/system-review</code> command analyzes the plugin's own performance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
          <p>AI agents make the same mistakes across sessions because they start fresh each time. The self-improving loop captures what went wrong (and right), encodes fixes as permanent CLAUDE.md rules, and tracks whether those fixes actually work over time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Performance Coach ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Performance Coach</h3>
    <p>The Performance Coach is an <strong>Opus agent</strong> that runs during <code>/wrap</code> at the end of every session. It analyzes what happened during the session and produces actionable coaching.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">What It Analyzes</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What It Checks</th><th>Example Finding</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Prompt Efficiency</strong></td>
            <td>Prompt churn (repeated similar prompts), tool loops (same tool called 3+ times)</td>
            <td>"You re-prompted the Builder 4 times for the same component &mdash; next time, include acceptance criteria in the spec."</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Cache Utilization</strong></td>
            <td>Cache hit ratio across the session</td>
            <td>"Cache utilization was 23% &mdash; consider keeping CLAUDE.md stable to improve prompt caching."</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Phase Completeness</strong></td>
            <td>Missing artifacts (tests, docs, review) for each phase</td>
            <td>"Feature shipped without E2E tests &mdash; consider adding /e2e to your workflow."</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Context Discipline</strong></td>
            <td>Context window usage at session end, number of compactions</td>
            <td>"Session hit 82% context usage &mdash; offload research to Stack Scout earlier."</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Anti-patterns</strong></td>
            <td>Recurring mistakes across 3+ sessions</td>
            <td>"This is the 3rd session with skipped code review &mdash; proposing a CLAUDE.md rule to make review mandatory."</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Vibe Score ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Vibe Score</h3>
    <p>Every session receives a <strong>quality score from 0 to 130</strong>. The score starts at 100 and applies deductions for problems and bonuses for excellence. The Performance Coach uses score trends to identify coaching opportunities.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Deductions</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>Penalty</th><th>What Triggers It</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Prompt churn</td><td>-5 per sequence</td><td>3+ similar prompts to the same agent without meaningful change</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tool loops</td><td>-10 per loop</td><td>Same tool called repeatedly without progress</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Low cache utilization</td><td>-15</td><td>Cache hit ratio below 30%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Context violations</td><td>-20</td><td>Session exceeded 60% context usage</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No tests</td><td>-10</td><td>Feature completed without any test files</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No feature spec</td><td>-5</td><td>Feature started without a spec in backlog.json</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Skipped code review</td><td>-5</td><td>Feature wrapped without running /review</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Documentation drift</td><td>-3 per stale doc</td><td>Source code changed but matching docs not updated</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Console errors</td><td>-5</td><td>Browser console errors on affected pages</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Visual token violations</td><td>-3 per violation (max -9)</td><td>Design system tokens used incorrectly</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Bonuses</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Achievement</th><th>Bonus</th><th>What Triggers It</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Complete phase artifacts</td><td>+5</td><td>All 6 phases have their required artifacts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>High cache utilization</td><td>+5</td><td>Cache hit ratio above 60%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Full test coverage</td><td>+5</td><td>All modified files have corresponding tests</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Clean session</td><td>+5</td><td>No tool loops, no prompt churn, no context violations</td></tr>
          <tr><td>TDD discipline</td><td>+3</td><td>Tests written before implementation code</td></tr>
          <tr><td>E2E tests passing</td><td>+3</td><td>Playwright E2E tests run and pass</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Accessibility clean</td><td>+2</td><td>No axe-core violations found</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Code review complete</td><td>+2</td><td>/review ran with all findings addressed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Performance baselines</td><td>+2</td><td>k6 performance tests run with baselines set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Visual compliance clean</td><td>+3</td><td>No design system token violations</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Session Learnings ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Session Learnings &amp; CLAUDE.md Mutations</h3>
    <p>When the Performance Coach detects a recurring anti-pattern (appears in 3+ sessions), it proposes a <strong>permanent rule change</strong> to the project's CLAUDE.md file. These mutations are the mechanism by which VibeCrew gets smarter over time.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">How Mutations Work</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What Happens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>1. Detection</strong></td><td>Performance Coach identifies a pattern appearing in 3+ sessions (e.g., "tests always written after code" or "docs consistently skipped").</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>2. Proposal</strong></td><td>Coach drafts a concrete CLAUDE.md rule using a standard template. Example: "Always run /tdd before implementing &mdash; write the test first."</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>3. Approval</strong></td><td>The proposal is shown to the user for approval. Mutations never auto-apply &mdash; the user always has final say.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>4. Application</strong></td><td><code>apply-mutation.sh</code> appends the rule to the Session Learnings section of CLAUDE.md.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>5. Pruning</strong></td><td>Session Learnings are capped at 15 entries. When a new rule is added past the limit, the oldest entry is removed to keep CLAUDE.md lean.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon amber">&#9888;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Why 15 learnings max?</strong>
          <p>Research shows oversized CLAUDE.md files increase agent cost 20%+ without improving success rates. The cap ensures the file stays under 200 lines. Old learnings that haven't prevented their anti-pattern get pruned first.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Cross-Project Telemetry ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Cross-Project Telemetry</h3>
    <p>VibeCrew tracks anonymized performance data across all your projects. This isn't sent anywhere &mdash; it lives locally in the plugin directory and powers the <code>/system-review</code> command.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">How It Works</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What Happens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Auto-registration</strong></td><td>During <code>/setup</code>, the project registers itself in the central plugin's <code>project-registry.json</code>. Project paths are replaced with anonymous aliases (project-001, project-002).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Data collection</strong></td><td>After each <code>/wrap</code>, anonymized metrics (Vibe Score, agent usage, token counts, cost) are appended to <code>telemetry/aggregate.json</code> in the plugin directory.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Aggregation</strong></td><td><code>/system-review</code> reads all registered projects' telemetry to compute cross-project trends: average scores, most-used agents, cost patterns, MCP adoption rates.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">What /system-review Shows</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What It Contains</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Score Trends</strong></td><td>Average Vibe Score trajectory across all projects &mdash; are you getting better over time?</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Agent Usage</strong></td><td>Which agents are used most, which are underutilized, and cost breakdown by agent.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cost Analysis</strong></td><td>Per-project and aggregate cost metrics. Identifies expensive patterns and cost-saving opportunities.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>MCP Adoption</strong></td><td>Which MCP servers are active across projects and their impact on session efficiency.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Ecosystem Research</strong></td><td>The System Reviewer agent checks for VibeCrew updates, new MCP servers, and community best practices.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Agent Persistent Memory ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Agent Persistent Memory</h3>
    <p>Some agents maintain <strong>persistent memory</strong> across sessions using two complementary mechanisms. This allows agents to learn from previous runs without relying solely on CLAUDE.md mutations.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Three Learning Mechanisms</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Storage</th><th>Scope</th><th>Used By</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Performance Coach Mutations</strong></td>
            <td>CLAUDE.md rules</td>
            <td>All agents (global)</td>
            <td>Performance Coach</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Expertise Records</strong></td>
            <td><code>.vibecrew/expertise/</code> JSON</td>
            <td>Cross-agent (structured)</td>
            <td>Builder, Code Reviewer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Agent Memory</strong></td>
            <td><code>.claude/agent-memory/</code></td>
            <td>Agent-private (free-form)</td>
            <td>Performance Coach, Code Simplifier, Security Auditor</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Why three mechanisms?</strong>
          <p>Each serves a different purpose: CLAUDE.md mutations encode <em>global rules</em> that all agents follow. Expertise records store <em>structured, queryable</em> data (conventions, failure patterns, decisions) shared across agents. Agent memory stores <em>private, free-form</em> notes for unstructured learnings specific to one agent.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Skill Evaluation ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>Skill Evaluation &amp; Model-Change Resilience</h3>
    <p>VibeCrew tracks whether its 34 skills remain effective as models improve. The <code>/validate-skills</code> command evaluates skill health, and model-change detection at session startup alerts you when re-evaluation is needed.</p>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">The Evaluation Loop</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What Happens</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>1. Detection</strong></td><td>Session startup compares the current model against <code>.last-validated-model.txt</code>. If changed, a one-line warning fires.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>2. Evaluation</strong></td><td><code>/validate-skills</code> runs 3-tier analysis: schema check, model-version tracking, and A/B test recommendations.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>3. Testing</strong></td><td>Capability-uplift skills (<code>analysis</code> + <code>action</code> categories) are flagged for A/B testing via Skill Creator.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>4. Tracking</strong></td><td>Results stored in <code>.vibecrew/reviews/skill-health-*.json</code> with model version and validation date.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Skill Categories</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Count</th><th>Maintenance Strategy</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>workflow</strong></td><td>7</td><td>Durable &mdash; encodes preferences, optimize triggers</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>analysis</strong></td><td>6</td><td>Capability-uplift &mdash; A/B test after model upgrades</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>action</strong></td><td>8</td><td>Capability-uplift &mdash; A/B test after model upgrades</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>dashboard</strong></td><td>4</td><td>Durable &mdash; read-only, optimize for speed</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>utility</strong></td><td>9</td><td>Durable &mdash; simple operations, minimal maintenance</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="key-points">
      <div class="key-point">
        <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Schema Validation Hook</strong>
          <p>The <code>validate-skill-schema.sh</code> PostToolUse hook automatically validates every SKILL.md write. It checks required fields, category values, and <code>context: fork</code> / <code>agent:</code> pairing &mdash; catching schema errors before they reach production.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Feedback Loop Diagram ── -->
  <section class="section">
    <h3>The Self-Improvement Loop</h3>
    <p>Every session feeds back into the system, making the next session more efficient. The loop runs automatically during <code>/wrap</code> &mdash; no manual intervention required.</p>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={selfImprovingDiagram} caption="How each session improves the next: score → coach → mutation → better sessions" />
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
